\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces A picture of a BPA in the isometric force test stand with components labeled.}}{40}{figure.caption.15}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces Results for finding the relationship between $F_{620}$ and $l_0$. (\textbf {a}) $F_{620_{10}}$ as a function of $l_0$, at $P_{620}$. Dashed line is the $F_{620_{10}}$ data from Festo. Solid line is the fit from Equation (\ref {eq:Fmax10}). (\textbf {b}) $F_{620_{20}}$ as a function of $l_0$ at $P_{620}$. Dashed line is $F_{620_{20}}$ data from Festo. Solid line is the fit from Equation (\ref {eq:Fmax20}).}}{42}{figure.caption.16}%
\contentsline {subfigure}{\numberline {(\normalfont (\textbf {a}))}{\ignorespaces {\centering }}}{42}{subfigure.2.1}%
\contentsline {subfigure}{\numberline {(\normalfont (\textbf {b}))}{\ignorespaces {\centering }}}{42}{subfigure.2.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces Results for finding the relationship between $F_{620}$, $l_0$, $P_{620}$, and $\epsilon _{620_{10}}$. (\textbf {a}) Isoclines of the surface fit for $F_{620_{10}}(l_0,P_{620})$. (\textbf {b}) $\epsilon _{620_{10}}$ versus $l_0$ at $P_{620}$. Although there is a general trend of longer resting lengths producing more contraction, no conclusive relationship between $\epsilon _{620}$ and $l_0$ could be deduced from this experiment. The error bars show the effect of being $\pm $\qty {1}{\mm } in measuring $l_0$ and $l_{620}$.}}{46}{figure.caption.18}%
\contentsline {subfigure}{\numberline {(\normalfont (\textbf {a}))}{\ignorespaces {\centering }}}{46}{subfigure.3.1}%
\contentsline {subfigure}{\numberline {(\normalfont (\textbf {b}))}{\ignorespaces {\centering }}}{46}{subfigure.3.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.4}{\ignorespaces Surface fit for $F^*(\epsilon ^*,P^*)$. Solid lines are from our model. Dashed lines from Festo supplied data. In total, 537 data points were collected and used to fit the 3D surfaces (321 data points for $\diameter $\qty {10}{\mm } BPAs and 216 data points for $\diameter $\qty {20}{\mm } BPAs). In total, 80\% of these data were used for training, and 20\% were used for validation. For figure clarity, not all data is included and plotted circles represent collected data at $\pm \qty {10}{\kPa }$ of the stated pressure. (\textbf {a}) Fit data for $\diameter $\qty {10}{\mm } Festo BPAs. (\textbf {b}) Fit data for $\diameter $\qty {20}{\mm } Festo BPAs.}}{47}{figure.caption.19}%
\contentsline {subfigure}{\numberline {(\normalfont (\textbf {a}))}{\ignorespaces {\centering }}}{47}{subfigure.4.1}%
\contentsline {subfigure}{\numberline {(\normalfont (\textbf {b}))}{\ignorespaces {\centering }}}{47}{subfigure.4.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.5}{\ignorespaces Comparison between measured and predicted BPA force at varying amounts of contraction and pressure. A $y=x$ perfect fit line is added for reference. The BPAs compared are: (\textbf {a}) $\diameter $\qty {10}{\mm }, $l_0=\qty {257}{\mm }$, (\textbf {b}) $\diameter $\qty {10}{\mm }, $l_0=\qty {233}{\mm }$, (\textbf {c}) $\diameter $\qty {20}{\mm }, $l_0=\qty {300}{\mm }$, and (\textbf {d}) $\diameter $\qty {20}{\mm }, $l_0=\qty {450}{\mm }$.}}{53}{figure.caption.21}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces \textbf {(A)} CAD solid model assembly of the revolute joint knee in the test stand. \textbf {(B)} 1-DoF pinned-joint robot knee in the test apparatus with important components labeled. Leg is shown with 30 degrees flexion, i.e. $\theta _\text {k} = \ang {-30}$. \textbf {(C)} Picture from isometric torque testing. Setup shows the revolute knee configured for a test of the extensor BPA. The knee is flexed with $\theta _\text {k} = \ang {-120}$. Also note the compressed shape of the inflated BPA on the knee during this high degree of flexion. }}{62}{figure.caption.22}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces ICR for biomimetic robot knee as a function of knee angle. Measured from $\theta _{1}$ on the tibia. Plot is overlayed on model of the knee at $\theta _\text {k}=0^\circ $ (solid line), and $-60^\circ $ and $-120^\circ $ (dotted dash lines). White circles represent measured points in $-15^\circ $ increments from $-150^\circ $ to $-15^\circ $, $-5^\circ $ increments from $-15^\circ $ to $0^\circ $, and then at $2^\circ $, $5^\circ $, and $10^\circ $.}}{64}{figure.caption.23}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces Two-rotation construction of the bracket frame}}{72}{figure.caption.24}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces Bracket frame orientations for: \textbf {(A)} flexor configuration on the pinned knee, \textbf {(B)} extensor configuration on the pinned knee, \textbf {(C)} flexor and extensor configurations on the biomimetic knee with $\diameter $\qty {10}{\mm } BPAs, and \textbf {(D)} flexor configuration on the biomimetic knee with the $\diameter $\qty {20}{\mm } BPA.}}{73}{figure.caption.25}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.5}{\ignorespaces Compliance projections and BPA wrapping geometry}}{80}{figure.caption.26}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.6}{\ignorespaces Pre-optimization pinned-knee torque versus knee angle for the four cross-validation flexor tests. Knee angle is negative in flexion, and flexion torque is negative. Gold circles are the hybrid calculation, which combines the measured muscle length and moment arm with the modeled force; indigo filled circles are experimentally measured torque; the gray dashed line is the rigid-body prediction; and the light orange dash-dot line is the unoptimized prediction, the improved BPA force characterization applied to the same rigid kinematics. Panel titles give the nominal resting length; the actual resting lengths are $l_{0}=\qty {48.5}{\cm }$ \textbf {(A)}, $l_{0}=\qty {41.0}{\cm }$ \textbf {(B)}, $l_{0}=\qty {45.7}{\cm }$ \textbf {(C)}, and $l_{0}=\qty {40.6}{\cm }$ with tendon \textbf {(D)}. Panels \textbf {(A)} and \textbf {(B)} are the training tests and panels \textbf {(C)} and \textbf {(D)} the validation tests of the adopted cross-validation fold.}}{89}{figure.caption.27}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.7}{\ignorespaces Optimized pinned-knee torque versus knee angle for the four cross-validation flexor tests, at the adopted correction terms (Table~\ref {tab:AdoptedXi}). Knee angle is negative in flexion, and flexion torque is negative. Blue filled circles are experimentally measured torque; the dashed light orange line is the unoptimized prediction of the improved BPA characterization; the magenta line is the optimized prediction of the corrected model. Panel titles give the nominal resting length; the actual resting lengths are $l_{0}=\qty {48.5}{\cm }$ \textbf {(A)}, $l_{0}=\qty {41.0}{\cm }$ \textbf {(B)}, $l_{0}=\qty {45.7}{\cm }$ \textbf {(C)}, and $l_{0}=\qty {40.6}{\cm }$ with tendon \textbf {(D)}. Panels \textbf {(A)} and \textbf {(B)} are the training tests and panels \textbf {(C)} and \textbf {(D)} the validation tests of the adopted cross-validation fold; the goodness-of-fit values are given in Table~\ref {tab:FlxPinGof}.}}{92}{figure.caption.29}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.8}{\ignorespaces Pinned-knee torque versus knee angle for the extensor BPAs: the six identification-pool tests at the adopted correction terms, with the extensor-specific terms $(\chi _{0}, \chi _{3})$ on the shared stiffness pair (Table~\ref {tab:AdoptedXi}). Knee angle is negative in flexion, and extension torque is positive. Gold circles are the hybrid calculation; indigo filled circles are experimentally measured torque; the dashed light orange line is the unoptimized prediction of the improved BPA characterization; the magenta line is the optimized prediction of the corrected model. Panel titles give the nominal resting length; the actual resting lengths are $l_{0}=\qty {43.6}{\cm }$ \textbf {(A)}, $l_{0}=\qty {43.2}{\cm }$ with tendon \textbf {(B)}, $l_{0}=\qty {45.7}{\cm }$ \textbf {(C)}, $l_{0}=\qty {40.0}{\cm }$ \textbf {(D)}, $l_{0}=\qty {40.0}{\cm }$ with tendon \textbf {(E)}, and $l_{0}=\qty {46.5}{\cm }$ \textbf {(F)}. Panels \textbf {(A)}--\textbf {(C)} are the training tests and panels \textbf {(D)}--\textbf {(F)} the held-out validation tests of the adopted cross-validation fold; the legend is given in panel \textbf {(A)}.}}{94}{figure.caption.31}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.9}{\ignorespaces Measured versus expected isometric torque results using a $\diameter $\qty {10}{mm}, $l_{0}=\qty {51.8}{\cm }$ extensor BPA on the biomimetic knee. Measured results are blue dots. Original prediction is a dashed black line. Optimized prediction is a solid magenta line. Hybrid calculation shown in yellow dots.}}{97}{figure.caption.33}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.10}{\ignorespaces A configuration that is particularly hard to model. The BPA passes between the femoral condyles close to ICR.}}{98}{figure.caption.34}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.11}{\ignorespaces Biomimetic-knee torque versus knee angle for the flexor BPAs. Knee angle is negative in flexion, and flexion torque is negative. \textbf {(A)} $\diameter $\qty {10}{\mm }, $l_{0}=\qty {41.5}{\cm }$ actuator with a \qty {21}{\mm } artificial tendon at \qty {604}{\kPa }. \textbf {(B)} $\diameter $\qty {20}{\mm }, $l_{0}=\qty {41.5}{\cm }$ actuator with a \qty {14}{\mm } artificial tendon at \qty {620}{\kPa }. Indigo filled circles are measured torque; the magenta line is the optimized prediction of the corrected model using the flexor terms identified on the pinned knee; the dashed light orange line is the unoptimized prediction of the improved BPA characterization alone. In \textbf {(B)}, the black dotted line is the maximum human isometric torque, and the pink circles are measured points at the reduced pressures \qtylist {560;421;325;281}{\kPa } (from left to right, respectively), recorded because full pressure would load the printed femur beyond its ultimate strength. \textbf {(C)} The redesigned two-BPA flexor route}}{100}{figure.caption.35}%
